Check reader-v1 RPA producer output, including shrink LRI, Coulomb, and GW
preprocessor input (`KPT.info`, `eband_vxc.txt`, and text LCAO wavefunctions),
for a compact two-atom H `1x1x2` solid.

For symmetry-reduced grids, reader-v1 appends the actual full-to-SCF mapping
to `librpa_bz_sample.txt` and writes spatial operations to
`OUT.<suffix>/symrot_R.txt`. These fixed references use the full grid;
temporary variants check symmetry 0 and 1.

Every file the RPA producer writes carries a `librpa_` prefix so users can
tell the LibRPA inputs apart from the rest of the OUT.* tree.
`stru_out.txt` is no longer produced: taesc-prep derives an equivalent
structure file from STRU/STRU_FINAL, `KPT.info` and `symrot_R.txt`.

The serial run compares stable producer references. The two-rank test checks
rank-owned output and compares KS binary assembly with independent text
wavefunctions from the same run, avoiding gauge-dependent historical data.
It also injects root-only and non-root file-open failures to check MPI abort.

## Preparing the LibRPA inputs

ABACUS no longer writes `stru_out.txt`. Run taesc-prep on the calculation
directory to produce it from `STRU`/`STRU_FINAL`, `KPT.info` and
`symrot_R.txt`:

```
taesc-prep /path/to/calculation
```

Requires taesc-prep `master` at or after the spatial-only symmetry-export
support (AESM-Group/taesc-prep#3), which is what makes `nspin 4` exports
readable. The command writes `stru_out.txt` and `bz_sample.txt` next to the
calculation inputs.

The converted directory must carry, before LibRPA reads it: the geometry
with its lattice vectors and Cartesian positions in Bohr, the reciprocal
lattice with 2*pi, every translation vector of the symmetry operations, and
for `nspin 4` the `spin_symmetry` block. `librpa_producer_manifest.json`
lists the remaining files this case requires and the tolerance each
comparison uses.

### Shifted k-grids

The converter must filter the exported operations against the actual sampled
points, not against an assumed Gamma-centred mesh. ABACUS exports the crystal
operations, so an operation can map the mesh onto itself when the grid is
unshifted and fail when the same mesh is sampled with an offset: inversion on
a `2x1x1` Gamma grid maps `{1/8, 5/8}` to `{7/8, 3/8}` only when the shift is
zero. taesc-prep derives the shift from the full-grid coordinates in
`KPT.info`; a converter that keeps only the mesh dimensions must reject
shifted grids rather than emit an over-permissive symmetry set.
